  {
    "candidate_id": "planning-002",
    "parent_id": "planning-000",
    "source_sha256": "31556410061483bcdf5593e3e4ee75db650bbfbab324a1b8fa833259e310b0d8",
    "origin": "model_proposed",
    "n_params": 3,
    "rationale": "Feedback shows the previous plan was fully legal but stopped after exactly one tighten_nut (7 actions, unsatisfied nut2/nut3, no failed actions). Cause: after tightening, the man is stranded at the nut location, and my spanner search required a successful walk to a spanner location, which failed (terminal location with no outgoing link), so the loop broke. Fix: (1) reverse BFS precomputes directed reachability so unreachable targets are detected before acting; (2) per nut, do an explicit round trip man->spanner->pickup->nut, never requiring a return walk from the nut location; (3) choose a spanner that is useable and reachable, preferring the one at/nearest the man's current location; (4) do not abort the whole plan when one nut cannot be tightened - continue with the rest. Also handle nuts already tight and goals other than tightened.",
